    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a93e9800-ab1f-549d-b594-34424359d8cc",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nConsider intervals where the coefficients of the derivative are nonzero.\n\n- **I.** $\\left(x+4\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\left(x+4\\right)\\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+1\\right)$\n- **II.** $\\left(x+4\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\left(x+4\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{y}+1\\right)$\n- **III.** $\\left(x+4\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\left(x+6\\right)\\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+1\\right)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(x+4\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\left(x+4\\right)\\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+1\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\left(x+4\\right)\\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+1\\right)}{\\left(x+4\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 1.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(x+4\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\left(x+4\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{y}+1\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\left(x+4\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{y}+1\\right)}{\\left(x+4\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt{y} + 1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\sqrt{y} + 1.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(x+4\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\left(x+6\\right)\\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+1\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\left(x+6\\right)\\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+1\\right)}{\\left(x+4\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\left(x + 6\\right) \\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 1\\right)}{x + 4}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
